// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  The Foreign Exchange — genuinely EVALUATE a λ-calculus Foreign Language Term: β-reduce it on
//  the f1r3node reducer and read back its normal form.
//
//  Run me with the RhoCalc interpreter:
//    rhocalc demos/flt-foreign-exchange/k-combinator.rho
//
//  A BARE TERM EVALUATES TO ITS NORMAL FORM
//  ────────────────────────────────────────
//  This program is a single λ-calculus Foreign Language Term (an `App` node — no send/receive), so
//  the interpreter EVALUATES it to its normal form: it seeds the term into the Lambda guest's own
//  in-Rho reduction engine and lets the β-reduction run to completion on the reducer, then reads
//  the resulting normal form off @"OUT" (and the ^fired ledger of rewrite firings).
//
//  THE COMPUTATION — the K combinator discards its second argument
//  ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  `lam`(fun, arg)`` reflects to the guest application node App(fun, arg). Here we build
//        App( App(K, I), K )
//  with the two standard combinators
//        K = lam a. lam b. a      — the constant combinator  (= λ.λ.1)   "keep the first argument"
//        I = lam x. x             — the identity combinator   (= λ.0)
//  so the whole term is the redex  (K I) K  =  "apply K to I, then apply the result to K".
//
//  It β-reduces in two steps, entirely in-Rho:
//        (K I) K   ⟶β   (λb. I) K   ⟶β   I
//  K takes two arguments and returns the FIRST (I), discarding the SECOND (K). The result is the
//  identity combinator.
//
//  EXPECT
//  ──────
//        normal form on @"OUT"  =  ⟦λ.0⟧          (the identity I — K kept its first argument)
//        ^fired ledger contains the "Beta" firings that performed the reduction
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

lam`((lam a. lam b. a, lam x. x), lam a. lam b. a)`
